<Docs title="Getting started" slug="getting-started" lede="From download to your first crossing, on two systems, in a few minutes." description="Install Daisy, allow it, pair two systems and cross your first edge.">
  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Two systems with Apple silicon, running macOS 26 or later.</li>
    <li>Both on the same network, wired or Wi-Fi.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Set up</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Download and install</h3>
        <p>Prefer Homebrew? Follow the <a href={href("/docs/user-guide/#homebrew")}>Homebrew installation guide</a>, then continue with opening Daisy below.</p>
        <p><a class="dl-only" href={downloadUrl}>Download Daisy {version}</a><span class="handoff-inline">Download Daisy {version} on your computer</span>, open the DMG, and drag Daisy into Applications. Do the same on the other system.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Open Daisy</h3>
        <p>Daisy lives in the menu bar. Its flower is gray until your systems are connected. Daisy opens at login from now on; turn off <strong>Open at login</strong> under <strong>Advanced…</strong> to stop that.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Allow Accessibility and Input Monitoring</h3>
        <p>macOS asks whether Daisy may read your keyboard and pointer. Daisy needs that to pass them to your other systems. It opens the right place in System Settings and waits until each permission is on. If macOS asks Daisy to reopen, click <strong>Reopen Daisy</strong>.</p>
        <p class="assurance">What you type goes only to systems you paired, encrypted on the way, and is never recorded. <a href={href("/security/#c-network")}>What Daisy sends</a></p>
        <p>Not working? Open Daisy, click <strong>Advanced…</strong> and use <strong>Set Up…</strong> beside the missing permission. <a href={href("/docs/user-guide/#troubleshooting")}>Troubleshooting</a></p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Pair the two systems</h3>
        <p>Keep both systems within reach. Click <strong>Start Sharing</strong> on both. They find each other automatically, and the system you started last shows a six-digit code. Type that code on the other system and click <strong>Connect</strong>. The code works once, and only for this pairing.</p>
            <p>If the systems do not find each other, click <strong>+</strong> beside <strong>Peers</strong> on one system, enter the other system’s local name or IP address, then click <strong>Save and Connect</strong>. Keep sharing on the other system. Existing connections stay active, and automatic discovery continues.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Arrange the screens</h3>
        <p>At the top of the Daisy window, drag the other system's screens to where they sit. Both systems keep one arrangement; the most recent change wins.</p>
        <img class="setup-shot" src={href("/screenshots/daisy-window.png")} width="1120" height="836" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The Daisy window: Laptop in control beside Studio's two displays, connected in 4 ms, with Studio listed under Peers." />
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <h3>Push through the edge</h3>
        <p>Move the pointer off a display toward the other system's. It appears there. Push back out to return. To add a third system, click <strong>Start Sharing</strong> on it, choose <strong>Add a System</strong> from the menu-bar flower on either of the first two, and type the code the new system shows. It joins both.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Keyboard combinations</h2>
<p>Keyboard combinations follow the pointer, including Shift, Control, Option, Command and Fn. Stopping sharing releases held remote input and closes every group connection.</p>

<h2>Take control back</h2>
  <p>Press <kbd>Control</kbd>-<kbd>Option</kbd>-<kbd>Command</kbd>-<kbd>Escape</kbd> on the system you are at. Control returns to it at once.</p>
  <p>Control never crosses while a mouse button is held, so a drag cannot end up stuck on the other system.</p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href("/docs/trust/")}>Trust and pairing</a>: choose how long your systems trust each other.</li>
    <li><a href={href("/docs/user-guide/")}>User guide</a>: everything else, troubleshooting, and the optional command line.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
